      "title": "Discovery & Delivery: Building the Right Thing Right",
      "module": "Product & Engineering Partnership",
      "estimatedMinutes": 26,
      "content": "<h2>Two Ways to Fail</h2><p>A technology organization can fail in two fundamentally different ways. It can build the wrong thing, software that is well-engineered but that customers do not want or that does not serve the business. Or it can build the right thing badly, the correct idea, poorly executed, unreliable, or so slow to arrive that the opportunity is lost. Both are failures, and they are different problems requiring different disciplines. Building the right thing is the work of discovery. Building it right is the work of delivery. A strong technology organization is genuinely good at both, and the CTO must care about both.</p><h2>The Cost of Building the Wrong Thing</h2><p>Of the two failures, building the wrong thing is often the more wasteful and the more invisible. An organization can spend months of its scarce engineering capacity building a feature with excellent engineering, ship it, and discover that almost no one uses it or that it does not move the business at all. All of that effort, all of that capacity, is simply gone. The painful part is that the engineering can be flawless and the outcome still a total waste. This is why discovery, the work of validating that something is worth building before building it fully, matters so much. Engineering excellence applied to the wrong thing is expensive, well-executed waste.</p><h2>What Discovery Is</h2><p>Discovery is the practice of reducing the risk that the organization builds the wrong thing. It asks, before significant engineering capacity is committed: is this a real problem customers have, will this solution actually solve it, will customers use and value it, and does it serve the business. Discovery uses lighter-weight, faster, cheaper methods to learn these answers, talking to customers, examining data, building small prototypes or experiments, releasing limited versions, before committing to full-scale delivery. The point of discovery is not bureaucracy; it is to fail cheap and fast on bad ideas so that the expensive delivery capacity is reserved for ideas that have genuine evidence behind them.</p><h2>Discovery and Delivery Run Together</h2><p>A common mistake is to imagine discovery and delivery as strictly sequential, a discovery phase, then a delivery phase. In a healthy organization they run continuously and in parallel: the organization is always doing some discovery on what might come next while delivering what has already been validated. Discovery feeds a steady stream of validated, de-risked work into delivery. This continuous model keeps delivery teams working on things that have genuine evidence behind them, rather than either guessing or sitting idle waiting for a discovery phase to finish.</p><h2>The CTO's Stake in Both</h2><p>It is sometimes assumed that discovery is purely product's job and engineering only does delivery. This is a mistake. Engineering has a genuine stake and a genuine contribution in discovery: engineers can rapidly prototype to test an idea, can identify which approaches are cheap and which are expensive, and care deeply about not having their capacity poured into things that turn out to be waste. The CTO should ensure engineering participates in discovery, and should hold the organization accountable for both disciplines: building the right thing, validated through real discovery, and building it right, through the delivery excellence covered throughout this program. An organization strong in only one of the two will still fail; the CTO must build strength in both.</p>",
